# A172 — the debug label that could never show (2026-09-15T15:29:14Z)

## 1. what the stylesheet says it is for
$ git show HEAD:web/style.css | grep -n 'debug label' -A 2
  570:/* A debug label, shown only when ?profile is in the URL. */
  571-.profile-badge {
  572-  position: fixed;
$ git show HEAD:web/style.css | grep -n '\[hidden\]'
  85:[hidden] { display: none !important; }
$ git show HEAD:web/index.html | grep -n 'profile-badge'
  169:<div id="profile-badge" class="profile-badge" hidden></div>
$ git show HEAD:web/app.js | grep -n 'profile-badge' -A 3
  172:    $("profile-badge").textContent =
  173-      screenInfo.matched
  174-        ? `${screenInfo.matched.name} · ${screenInfo.matched.width}×${screenInfo.matched.height}`
  175-        : `${screenInfo.width}×${screenInfo.height} · unknown device`;
  (the badge is hidden in markup, the stylesheet makes [hidden] unstoppable, and the page
   only ever wrote textContent — so the documented ?profile condition had no implementation)

## 2. before: the check on the wiring
$ node tools/check-web-deltas.js   # with the badge handling removed again
    ok    every snapshot the page applies goes through the guard
    FAIL  the debug label reads ?profile
    FAIL  and the badge is shown only when it is asked for
  2 deltas check(s) failed

## 3. after
$ grep -n 'profileRequested' web/app.js
  180:    badge.hidden = !profileRequested();
  184:  function profileRequested() {
$ node tools/check-web-deltas.js
    ok    the debug label reads ?profile
    ok    and the badge is shown only when it is asked for
  all deltas checks passed

  Limit, stated rather than implied: the browser behaviour itself is not exercised — this audit
  has no headless browser — so what is checked is that the page reads ?profile and applies it.
